        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What storm season does to a roofer's phone</h2>
        <p>
          Northwest Arkansas has humid subtropical summers, a spring tornado
          season, and winter ice events. The week this page is about is the
          spring storm week, when one hail line fills the board. The crew is
          already on a roof. The phone does not stop.
        </p>
        <p>
          The calls are not one kind of call. Some are storm damage. Some are
          water still coming in. Some are "is my roof okay" with no water, and
          some are estimate requests from people who drove past a neighbor's
          tarp. If every one of those pages the on-call crew, the emergency
          list is useless by the second day. If none of them get answered, the
          estimates book with whoever picked up.
        </p>
        <p>
          The setup answers in your business's voice and sorts those calls by
          rules you wrote before the week arrived. It does not add crews. It
          does not tell a caller a truck is rolling when your list says it is
          not. We are not publishing a hail count or a tornado count for this
          corridor. This page does not have one.
        </p>
        <p>
          An industry analysis cites Arkansas among the highest-demand states
          for HVAC AI answering
          (<a class="text-link" href="https://enumsol.com/ai-answering-for-hvac" rel="noopener">Enumsol, HVAC AI answering</a>).
          That is their analysis of HVAC demand, not a count of NWA roofing
          tickets, and it is not a promise about your next storm week. The
          adjacent point is the surge: when weather drives call volume in this
          state, the phone fails the same way whether the trade is cooling or
          roofing. We do not borrow their HVAC figure as a roofing result.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What counts as an emergency on a roofing call</h2>
        <p>
          You write the line before the first storm call. The setup does not
          move a call across that line on its own. A roofing call on this page
          starts from two lines in the contractor emergency set, and does not
          add a third. You can make a line stricter. We will not loosen one
          with a hail size, a wind speed, or a square count we made up.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Storm damage.</strong> An opening, or water coming in from the storm, reaches the on-call crew. A quote for a roof that is not taking water books a look. It does not page someone at 11 p.m. We do not add a hail size or a wind speed.</li>
          <li><strong>An active leak.</strong> Water that is still moving and wetting floors, cabinets, or the space below is an emergency. If the caller has already contained it and nothing is still wetting anything, it stays a booking if that is the rule you wrote. The setup asks. It does not guess.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The other two lines on the contractor page, no heat below 40°F and a
          gas smell, are not roofing lines. This page does not borrow them. If
          a caller reached the roofing number with a problem that is not storm
          damage and not an active leak, it books the next look or takes a
          message. A call that does not match a rule you approved does not get
          a guessed emergency.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those two lines, and the two this page leaves alone, are written out
          on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/after-hours-answering-service-contractors-nwa/">after-hours answering page for NWA contractors</a>.
          If the caller is a property manager sending you to a unit, their side
          of that handoff is on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/property-management-answering-service-northwest-arkansas/">property management answering page</a>.
          You still only roll to the address and the crew list you already agreed to.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How 60-second dispatch to the on-call crew works</h2>
        <p>
          When a call matches an emergency rule you approved, the dispatch goes
          out within 60 seconds. The handoff carries the caller's name, callback
          number, address, and the problem in the words the triage questions
          captured. It is not a "call the office" page with none of that. Sixty
          seconds is the page going out. It is not a promise the crew picked up,
          and it is not a measured average from our accounts.
        </p>
        <p>
          Who gets the page is a drive-time question, not one phone for the
          whole metro. You name the zones. A call in south Fayetteville and a
          call in Bentonville are not the same crew when your people are spread
          along I-49 and Highway 71 between Fayetteville and Bentonville. The
          setup uses the address against the zones you wrote. It does not send
          the Fayetteville crew north because they were next on a single list,
          unless that is the rule you wrote. The Bentonville end of that
          corridor has its own page at
          <a class="text-link" href="/ai-receptionist-bentonville-ar/">AI receptionist in Bentonville, AR</a>.
          We are not publishing a minute table for the drive. We did not measure
          one for this page. You set the boundaries from the drives your crews
          already know.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the on-call crew does not accept, the backup you named is next. If
          nobody accepts, the manager you named gets the same facts. We do not
          keep a crew roster of our own, and we do not send a contractor you
          did not name. Every emergency call is recorded and transcribed, the
          same as every other call.
        </p>
        <p>
          A non-emergency does not use this path. If the first ring was missed
          because the crew was in the truck, that is a different problem,
          covered on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/missed-call-text-back-northwest-arkansas/">missed call text back page</a>.
          A text is not a substitute for the emergency page.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Booking estimates and inspections into ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, and Jobber</h2>
        <p>
          A roof that is not taking water should not become a voicemail you
          return after the storm week. It gets a real slot for an estimate or
          an inspection on the calendar the shop already opens.
        </p>
        <p>
          ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, and Jobber are the three we book into
          when that is the system you run. The appointment, the address, the
          job type, and the notes the script captured land on that calendar, so
          the office is not retyping a transcript at 7 a.m. We do not ask you
          to switch tools to get storm-week booking, and we do not claim a
          certified native sync into any of the three. If a field the tool needs
          is not in the script, it does not get invented.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a job needs a look before a price, it books the look. The setup
          quotes only a range you put in the script, or nothing. It does not
          invent a ticket total on a roof nobody has seen. "Is my roof okay"
          with no water coming in is that look, or a message, depending on the
          rule you wrote. It is not an emergency page.
        </p>
        <p>
          The calendar layer, separate from the phone, is the
          <a class="text-link" href="/booking-software/">booking software</a>
          setup. It does not replace the office manager who owns the board in
          the morning.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Surge capacity: the roster you write before the week hits</h2>
        <p>
          Surge capacity is not a bigger phone script. It is the roster, written
          before the week hits. You name who is on call, who is backup, and
          which stretch of I-49 and Highway 71 between Fayetteville and
          Bentonville they cover. A shop with one crew and a shop with several
          do not have the same bench. The price does not change with the bench.
          The list does.
        </p>
        <p>
          One hail line can fill that board in a day. Storm-damage calls and
          active leaks still use the emergency path. The rest of the spring
          calls book a look or take a message. If they all page the on-call
          crew, the emergency list is useless by the second day. The setup
          does not invent extra crews. It uses the list you gave it, including
          the overflow path when the first crew is already on a roof. If that
          path is a detailed message and a text to the owner, that is what the
          caller gets. We will not tell them a crew is rolling when it is not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Humid subtropical summers and winter ice are on the same calendar.
          They can change how many calls arrive at once. They do not add a new
          emergency line on this page. Spring tornado season is the week the
          roster is for. If you want a different rotation for a summer week or
          an ice week, you write that rotation ahead of time. We do not add it
          because the weather changed.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Bilingual crews and callers</h2>
        <p>
          Northwest Arkansas has an English and Spanish caller base, and the
          crews covering these roofs are bilingual too. A setup that only
          handles English is not answering for the calls you actually get. A
          dispatch that does not note the caller's language can send a crew
          that cannot finish the conversation on site.
        </p>
        <p>
          What our build can and cannot do in Spanish, today, is something we
          demonstrate on the assessment call rather than promise in print.
          Bring a real call type. You will hear it. If you want the page to say
          the caller was speaking Spanish, that is a question in the script you
          approve, and the crew you named for those calls is the one who gets
          it. We do not invent a translation of a diagnosis the script was not
          written to capture.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Pricing</h2>
        <p>
          One published price sheet, the same everywhere in Arkansas. Crew
